The largest cube will have one vertex at the origin, and its opposite vertex on the sphere $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 25.$

[asy]
import three;

size(180);
currentprojection = perspective(6,3,2);

triple I = (1,0,0), J = (0,1,0), K = (0,0,1);
triple O = (0,-0.2,0), P = (sqrt(12.5),sqrt(12.5),sqrt(12.5));

draw(surface(sphere(5)),paleyellow,nolight);
draw(sphere(5));
draw((P + 0.2*(P - O))--(P + 0.2*(P - O) + 0.1*(O - P))--(P + 0.1*(O - P)));
draw((0,-5,0)--(0,5,0));
draw((0,0,-1)--(0,0,6));
draw((-5,0,0)--(5,0,0));
draw(O--P,red,Arrow3(6));
draw(O--(sqrt(12.5),sqrt(12.5),0)--P--(sqrt(12.5),0,sqrt(12.5))--cycle);
draw((0,0,0)--(sqrt(12.5),0,0),dashed);
draw((0,0,0)--(0,sqrt(12.5),0),dashed);
draw((sqrt(12.5),0,0)--(sqrt(12.5),sqrt(12.5),0),dashed);

label("$x$", (5.2,0,0));
label("$y$", (0,5.2,0));
label("$z$", (0,0,6.2));
label("$O$", O, S);
label("$P$", P, N);
[/asy]

Let the side length of the cube be $s.$  Then the opposite vertex of the cube is $(s,s,s),$ so
\[s^2 + s^2 + s^2 = 25.\]This simplifies to $3s^2 = 25,$ so $s^2 = \frac{25}{3}.$  Since $s$ is positive, $s = \boxed{\frac{5 \sqrt{3}}{3}}.$
